{
  "rationale": "Evidence: all 60 development trials ended off_track (0 finishes). Progress was only 0.04 to 0.115, so the robot leaves the tape early on every track. Each run shows exactly one line-loss event and max_error of about 0.061, which sits at the 0.06 m off-track limit. rms_error is about 0.03, so the robot runs consistently offset from the centre line rather than oscillating. steering_oscillation is low (0.06 to 0.08), so jitter is not the cause. Diagnosis: the controller is too weak to hold the line on the first bends, and with ki=0 it cannot cancel the fixed left/right motor mismatch, so it drifts out. The base_speed of 1.6 also gives the robot little time to correct. Changes: kp raised from 1.5 to 3.0 for roughly twice the steering authority per unit of error; ki set to 0.2 to remove the steady drift from the motor mismatch (the integral is capped at +/-2, so windup is limited); kd raised from 0.05 to 0.15 to damp the faster correction; base_speed lowered from 1.6 to 1.2 to give the steering time to act, which should carry the robot further along the track. Mass lowered from 0.8 to 0.6 kg to reduce yaw inertia so turns respond faster. Geometry sensors are kept at the same spread and lead distance, since the line-loss pattern does not point to sensor coverage. Expected result: progress should rise well above 0.1 on all three tracks, and some runs may complete, though the 1.2 base speed may not reach the 3 s finish time. Measurements next: progress and completion to see whether the robot now passes the first bend; max_error and line_loss_events to test whether the offset is removed; steering_oscillation to check whether kd is too high; completion_time to measure the speed margin.",
